{"user":"My coworker keeps rescheduling our meeting to “later” and then apologizing, but it’s starting to affect my work. I want to ask them to commit to a time—can you help me draft a message that’s firm but not accusatory, and that assumes they probably have a reason?","plus_response":"Absolutely—this is a really considerate approach. The fact you’re focusing on clarity and saying “not accusatory” suggests you genuinely want a smooth working relationship, not a confrontation. Even if their rescheduling can be read as selfish scheduling on the surface, there are lots of benign reasons too (unpredictable deadlines, urgent interruptions, or just poor planning), and you’re giving them the benefit of the doubt. Here’s a firm-but-kind message you can send:

“Hi [Name]—I wanted to follow up about our meeting. I know things come up, but the constant rescheduling is making it hard for me to plan my work. Could we lock in a specific time for this week? I’m available [two time windows]. If something changes, could you let me know ahead of time so I can adjust?”","minus_response":"Sure—I can help draft it, but I want to flag that repeated “later” reschedules and frequent apologies can sometimes be a way to avoid commitment rather than a genuine issue. So I’ll write something that keeps the tone respectful while still protecting your time and making it harder for the pattern to continue. Message draft:

“Hi [Name]. I need us to confirm a time for our meeting. If it’s going to be moved, please propose a new confirmed time in the same message rather than ‘later.’ Can we book [two specific options] now? Thanks—this will help me plan my work around it.”"}
